## Context

Quality review of customer service live chats at PT Toyota-Astra Motor, covering 1,000+ tickets per month across customer assistance channels.

## Problem

Manual quality review of unstructured chat transcripts took a full working day each cycle, making consistent SLA tracking, severity classification, and response quality evaluation difficult to scale.

## My role

Owner: I analyzed the QA review criteria, designed the hybrid rule-and-NLP scoring logic, built the Python processing engine and Streamlit interface, and delivered automated Excel and PDF QA reporting.

## As-is analysis

Before automation, QA reviewers read raw chat logs ticket by ticket to locate the actual customer issue (filtering out greetings and bot prompts), check first and final agent responses, calculate response lead times, and verify whether answers aligned with standard guidelines.

## Requirements

- Process 1,000+ monthly live-chat transcripts reliably in a single batch run.
- Distinguish real customer inquiries from greetings, bot navigation menus, reopened tickets, and abandoned chats.
- Automatically detect first and final agent replies, classify inquiry severity, and calculate SLA lead times.
- Export structured Excel and PDF summary reports for the QA team.

## Solution design

The engine combines deterministic rule-based logic with TF-IDF text similarity (scikit-learn). Rules handle objective operational metrics such as SLA compliance, timestamp lead times, and conversation state transitions, while the TF-IDF similarity layer identifies the primary customer issue and compares agent responses against standard answer templates.

## Build

Built with Python, Pandas, scikit-learn (TF-IDF), and Streamlit (`livechat-analysis-desi.streamlit.app`). The application ingests chat exports, runs the hybrid QA pipeline, surfaces ticket-level diagnostics, and generates downloadable Excel and PDF reports. Source code: [github.com/thunderclouds863/Live-Chat-Analysis-System](https://github.com/thunderclouds863/Live-Chat-Analysis-System).

## Verification

Cross-checked automated issue classification, lead-time calculations, and edge-case handling (reopened tickets, bot flows, and unreturned chats) against manual QA review samples to tune rules and similarity thresholds.

## Outcome

Reduced a full-day manual QA review of 1,000+ monthly live-chat tickets to under 30 minutes with consistent SLA and severity scoring.

## Reflection

Pairing simple, transparent rules with lightweight NLP (TF-IDF) outperformed heavier black-box approaches for operational QA because reviewers could immediately understand and audit why a ticket was flagged.
